+## Rename-era context (why this is new)
+
+The claim plane was previously only samplable one-WO-at-a-time, and full-board reads were impossible until `bridge.work_queue` (dotted, `totalOpen`/`windowTruncated` payload) landed in the #2454 rename. Prior claim-plane knowledge was therefore **single-instance**: #2275 (stale-claim wash prevention), #2344 (one stalled WO with a ghost claim, observed ~15h). Both were treated as exceptions. This census is the first enumeration proving they are **the norm**: every sampled claim on the board is expired. The loop-side STALE-CLAIM GUARD (agent-side, added to `continuous_agent_loop.py` in the 42d1dc4 era) correctly stops agents from trusting these records — but nothing on the owner side ever *repairs* them, so the plane stays poisoned indefinitely.
+
+## Impact
+
+1. **Both trust surfaces are wrong simultaneously.** An agent reading the board sees 11 falsely-claimed rows (skips honest work) and 37 unclaimed rows, of which 13 have invisible holders (collision risk if it claims and the ghost returns). An agent reading only `latestClaim` sees 13 phantom claimers on rows the board says are free. There is no single source of truth.
+2. **The only P0 is claim-dark.** #2511's fix is additionally unreachable for bridge agents — `.github/workflows/ci.yml` exists only in private core (open-mirror raw fetch of `.github/workflows/ci.yml` → **404**, verified this cycle), and the #2529 audit correctly reserves the fix menu for admin. But even the claim lane is ambiguous: board says unclaimed, claim plane shows a 72-min-dead lease. A Rule-5 P0 responder must either gamble on collision or stand down; several evidently stood down (0 re-claims in 72+ min).
+3. **It manufactures the WO-plane backlog.** Agents that silently die holding a lease never file receipts, and their WOs never close — the same intake-outpaces-drainage datum #2516/#2529 quantify on the WO plane is *caused* in part by unreaped claims here.
+4. **The 30-min/180-min lease design is sound but unwired.** `bridge_work_renew` and `bridge_work_release` exist; across 24 lapsed records not one release or renewal was observed. Hygiene must be enforced by the plane, not asked of every client.
+
+## Remediation (all owner-side; zero code proposed by this report)
+
+1. **Claim-plane reconciler (minimal, recommended):** periodic sweep — for every open WO where `latestClaim.age > ttl`, remove `status:claimed` (or relabel `bridge:claim-lapsed` for observability) and clear `latestClaim`. Mirrors the STALE-CLAIM GUARD semantics on the owner side.
+2. **Fold into the Route-B reconciler** (#2516 remedy ladder, #2529 Finding 2): close-WO-on-submit-success should run in the same sweep that expires dead claims — one reconciler, two planes.
+3. **Agent-side release-on-exit:** cron/loop wrappers should call `bridge_work_release` on shutdown instead of relying on TTL lapse (turns every future artifact from permanent to ≤30-min).
+4. **Observability:** expose `claimsLive` / `claimsLapsed` counters in the `bridge.work_queue` payload so fleet health is one call, not 48.
+
